      "path": "README.md",
      "content": "# strict-spec-driven\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n**Documentation:** [Quick Start](QUICKSTART.md) · [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) · [Architecture Quickstart](ARCHITECTURE_QUICKSTART.md) · [UI Design Quickstart](DESIGN_QUICKSTART.md) · [Roadmap Quick Start](ROADMAP_QUICKSTART.md) · [Roadmap Guide](ROADMAP_GUIDE.md) · [AGENTS](AGENTS.md)\n\n`strict-spec-driven` is a YAML-first spec-driven development workflow. It provides the spec-driven lifecycle (propose → apply → verify → skill-driven code review → archive) with Agent Skills integration, converting workflow artifacts — specs, changes, roadmap, tasks, questions — into strict YAML schemas enforced by deterministic scripts.\n\n`SKILL.md` files remain Markdown because that is the Agent Skills packaging format requirement. What changes is the artifact format those skills generate and consume.\n\n## Directory Structure\n\n| Directory | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `.strict-spec-driven/` | Active strict workflow state |\n| `.strict-spec-driven/changes/` | Active change directories |\n| `.strict-spec-driven/changes/archive/` | Archived changes |\n| `.strict-spec-driven/roadmap/` | Strict YAML roadmap artifacts |\n| `.strict-spec-driven/roadmap/planned-changes/` | Preferred standalone roadmap work-item files |\n| `.strict-spec-driven/specs/` | Strict YAML main spec files |\n| `templates/strict-spec-driven/schemas/` | Bundled strict YAML schema files |\n| `examples/strict-spec-driven/` | Repository reference minimal valid YAML examples |\n| `templates/strict-spec-driven/scaffold/` | Scaffold templates used by `init` and `propose` |\n| `skills/` | Strict skill packages (`strict-spec-*` and `strict-roadmap-*`) |\n| `src/` | TypeScript CLI source |\n| `dist/` | Compiled output (generated by build) |\n\nThis repository keeps a single active workflow directory, `.strict-spec-driven/`. A `.spec-driven/` directory is only relevant as migration *input* for other projects that still use the legacy Markdown workflow — see [Migration Boundaries](#migration-boundaries). This repository completed that migration and retains no root `.spec-driven/` directory.\n\n## Upgrading\n\n<!-- retired-skill-names -->\n\nThe nine single-command roadmap skills were merged into one parameterized skill,\nand `/strict-roadmap-reorder` was then split back out because it is invoked often\nenough to earn a dedicated entry point. `/strict-roadmap-pause`,\n`/strict-roadmap-resume`, `/strict-roadmap-defer`, `/strict-roadmap-refine`,\n`/strict-roadmap-move`, `/strict-roadmap-reopen`, `/strict-roadmap-archive`, and\n`/strict-roadmap-sync` no longer exist as skill packages, and no alias package\nforwards them, so an invocation of one of those slash commands now fails rather\nthan silently doing the right thing.\n\nThe five skills `/strict-spec-sop`, `/strict-spec-harness-init`,\n`/strict-spec-harness-session`, `/strict-spec-migrate`, and\n`/strict-spec-propose-from-roadmap` were removed the same way. The `sop-*`\ncommands and `migrate` are unchanged — they keep their names, arguments, and\nbehavior, so those capabilities remain available through `strict-spec-cli` — and\nroadmap-backed proposals are scaffolded by `/strict-roadmap-recommend` when the\nnext change is chosen across the whole roadmap, or by `/strict-spec-propose`\nwhen the request names one milestone.\n\nEach retired roadmap name is now an explicit operation of one skill, except\nreorder, which has its own skill again:\n\n| Retired slash command | Replacement |\n|---|---|\n| `/strict-roadmap-pause` | `/strict-roadmap-transition pause` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-resume` | `/strict-roadmap-transition resume` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-defer` | `/strict-roadmap-transition defer` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-refine` | `/strict-roadmap-transition refine` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-reorder` | `/strict-roadmap-reorder` (its own skill again) |\n| `/strict-roadmap-move` | `/strict-roadmap-transition move` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-reopen` | `/strict-roadmap-transition reopen` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-archive` | `/strict-roadmap-transition archive` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-sync` | `/strict-roadmap-transition sync` |\n\nThe underlying CLI commands are unchanged: `roadmap-pause`, `roadmap-sync`, and\nthe rest keep their names and arguments. Only the skill packaging changed.\n\nThe harness capability was removed entirely, because declaring, starting,\nstopping, and logging project services lies outside a spec-driven workflow tool.\nThe `harness-edit`, `harness-infer`, `harness-doctor`, `harness-plan`,\n`harness-start`, `harness-stop`, `harness-status`, `harness-logs`,\n`harness-scripts`, and `harness-session` commands are no longer registered and\nfail as unknown commands; `init` no longer scaffolds `harness.yaml`; and the MCP\nserver no longer offers harness tools, the `strict://harness/declaration`\nresource, or the `strict_harness_investigation` prompt. No command reads the\nfiles an earlier version left behind, so `.strict-spec-driven/harness.yaml`,\n`.strict-spec-driven/harness-sessions/`, and generated `scripts/harness-*`\nwrappers can be deleted by hand.\n\nIf you installed the skills before this change, reinstall them so the retired\npackages are removed from your skill directory and the merged one is present:\n\n```bash\nbun run install-strict-skills\n```\n\n## Strict Command Surface\n\n### CLI: `strict-spec-driven`\n\nSubcommands available after `bun run build` (or via installed `node dist/strict-spec-cli.js`):\n\n| Subcommand | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `init` | Scaffold `.strict-spec-driven/` in a project |\n| `propose <name>` | Create a new strict change scaffold |\n| `apply <name>` | Complete the next pending task in a change |\n| `verify <name>` | Validate a change's artifacts and task completion |\n| `archive <name>` | Merge delta specs and move change to archive |\n| `ship <name>` | Read archived change summary for git commit handoff |\n| `migrate` | Convert `.spec-driven/` bootstrap state to `.strict-spec-driven/` |\n| `list` | Report active and archived changes |\n| `modify [<name>]` | List editable artifact paths for a change |\n| `cancel <name>` | Remove an active change (archived changes are protected) |\n| `maintenance` | Run configured repository maintenance checks |\n| `roadmap-status` | Validate and report strict roadmap state |\n| `roadmap-overview` | Print a human-readable roadmap overview table |\n| `roadmap-sync [--fix-names]` | Reconcile and regenerate strict roadmap index; `--fix-names` renames misnamed planned-change files to canonical names |\n| `roadmap-generate milestone` | Create or update a strict roadmap milestone through structured named arguments |\n| `roadmap-generate planned-change` | Create or update a standalone planned-change artifact at its canonical CLI-computed filename |\n| `validate-skills` | Validate strict skill packaging metadata and command symlinks |\n| `schema-list` | List bundled strict schema asset keys |\n| `schema-show <name>` | Print a bundled strict schema asset |\n| `sample-list` | List bundled strict YAML sample asset keys |\n| `sample-show <name>` | Print a bundled strict YAML sample asset |\n| `template-list` | List bundled template and schema asset keys |\n| `template-show <key>` | Print a bundled template or schema asset |\n\n### Installation\n\nRun `install.sh` on Unix-like hosts or `install.ps1` on Windows. The installer installs the strict skills package and the `strict-spec-cli` command, and bootstraps its own dependencies: `bun` is installed automatically when missing, project dependencies are installed with `bun install` when `node_modules` is absent, and the global `skills` CLI runs through `bunx`.\n\nOn Unix-like hosts, `install.sh` installs `strict-spec-cli` via `npm install -g .` when npm/Node is available and the npm global prefix is writable — the command lands in npm's global bin directory (already on `PATH`), so it is usable immediately with no PATH edits and no large native executable. When npm is unavailable, or `npm install -g .` fails on permissions (a system-managed global prefix that would need sudo), it falls back to building a native `strict-spec-cli` executable into a user-level bin directory (`~/.local/bin` by default, or `$STRICT_SPEC_BIN_DIR` / `$XDG_BIN_HOME`). On a successful npm install it also removes any native executable left by a prior fallback install, so only one `strict-spec-cli` resolves on `PATH`.\n\nOn Windows, `install.ps1` installs `strict-spec-cli` via `npm install -g .` when npm/Node is available — the command lands in npm's global bin directory (already on `PATH`), so it is usable immediately in any terminal with no PATH edits. When npm is unavailable, it falls back to building a native `strict-spec-cli` executable and placing it in a user-level bin directory; in that case it adds the bin directory to the current user's `PATH` by default, so open a new terminal before invoking `strict-spec-cli` by name outside the installer session. Use `-NoPathUpdate` to skip PATH changes, or `-KeepPathOnUninstall` to leave the PATH entry in place when uninstalling.\n\nBoth installers also remove the skills this repository has retired. Installing only ever adds packages, so upgrading over an older checkout used to leave every deleted skill on the machine, still offering a slash command that no longer resolves. The retired names are recorded in `retired-skills.json` — they cannot be derived from a name prefix, because some retired packages predate the current naming families — and each install hands that list to the `skills` CLI, which removes them from every agent directory it manages. Uninstalling names both the packaged and the retired skills for the same reason: given no names, the `skills` CLI opens an interactive selection menu, which a non-interactive session answers with nothing at all.\n\n### Skills: `strict-spec-*`, `strict-roadmap-*`, and `strict-scene-*`\n\nSkills are Agent Skills packages installed via `install.sh`. Invoke them in Claude Code using their slash-command names:\n\n**Strict spec workflow skills** (`strict-spec-*`):\n\n| Slash command | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/strict-spec-init` | Initialize strict workflow in a project |\n| `/strict-spec-propose` | Propose a new change, standalone or scoped to a named roadmap milestone |\n| `/strict-spec-apply` | Implement tasks in a change |\n| `/strict-spec-verify` | Verify a change is complete |\n| `/strict-spec-review` | Evidence-based code-quality review of a change |\n| `/strict-spec-archive` | Archive a completed change |\n| `/strict-spec-auto` | End-to-end automated change lifecycle |\n| `/strict-spec-ship` | Ship an archived change via git commit |\n| `/strict-spec-brainstorm` | Brainstorm a change idea before proposing |\n| `/strict-spec-modify` | Modify an existing change's artifacts |\n| `/strict-spec-spec-edit` | Edit main spec files directly |\n| `/strict-spec-sync-specs` | Synchronize specs with code drift |\n| `/strict-spec-design` | Author or refresh the design system artifact and render `DESIGN.md` |\n| `/strict-spec-design-sync` | Reconcile the design system artifact with UI code drift |\n| `/strict-spec-maintenance` | Run repository maintenance workflow |\n\n**Strict roadmap skills** (`strict-roadmap-*`):\n\n| Slash command | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/strict-roadmap-recommend` | Recommend the next planned change from the roadmap |\n| `/strict-roadmap-review` | Review one incomplete roadmap milestone and apply fixes |\n| `/strict-roadmap-plan` | Create or restructure the roadmap |\n| `/strict-roadmap-transition` | Perform one roadmap state transition: `pause`, `resume`, `defer`, `refine`, `move`, `reopen`, `archive`, or `sync` |\n| `/strict-roadmap-reorder` | Change one active milestone's position in roadmap order |\n\n**Strict scene skills** (`strict-scene-*`):\n\n| Slash command | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/strict-scene-edit` | Author or correct a scene: actors, preconditions, main flow, exception flows, and each step's coverage |\n\nThe roadmap docs describe the preferred split-file collaboration model: keep milestone files and `INDEX.yaml` low churn, store day-to-day roadmap work in `.strict-spec-driven/roadmap/planned-changes/`, rerun `roadmap-sync` after merges or archive handoff, and treat external tracker references as optional context rather than required source of truth.\n\nStandalone planned-change files use a canonical, CLI-owned filename: `<milestone-id>-<planned-change-id>.yaml` (when the planned-change id already starts with the milestone id, the prefix is not duplicated). Filename order is semantically meaningful — `roadmap-recommend` selects the first eligible planned change in filename order — so an active planned-change file with a non-canonical basename is a blocking validation diagnostic in `roadmap-status` and `roadmap-sync` (archived planned-change files are exempt). Create and update planned-change artifacts through `roadmap-generate planned-change`, which computes the filename itself, and repair misnamed legacy files with `roadmap-sync --fix-names`; never pick planned-change filenames by hand.\n\n## Requirement Interrogation\n\nEvery path that starts from a human request — `/strict-spec-brainstorm`,\n`/strict-spec-propose`, `/strict-spec-bugfix`, `/strict-spec-auto` — interrogates\nthe *need* before the change-intake gate interrogates the *solution*. It runs\nfirst because a well-scoped change built against a misread need is still the\nwrong change.\n\nRequirement answers are never taken from repository evidence. Code shows what\nthe system does; it cannot show what a person wants. Only the requester's own\nwords — including what they already said in the request — or replayed roadmap\nplanned-change and milestone detail may answer them.\n\nThe first `generate <change-name> questions ...` invocation seeds five canonical\nquestions into `questions.yaml` as open entries, so a newly authored change is\nblocked until they are answered:\n\n| Question id | Asks | A sufficient answer contains |\n|---|---|---|\n| `requirement-problem` | What is concretely going wrong today, and for whom? | Who is affected, what they do today instead, and how often — without naming a feature |\n| `requirement-outcome` | What observable difference proves it is solved? | What a person will see or no longer have to do, checkable from outside the system |\n| `requirement-trigger` | Why now — what forced this? | The concrete event, deadline, incident, or cost, or an honest statement that nothing forced it |\n| `requirement-non-goals` | What must explicitly not change? | The nearby behavior that must survive untouched, and who relies on it |\n| `requirement-interpretation` | Which reading is intended? | The intended reading stated plainly enough to rule the others out |\n\nEach seeded entry carries its own explanation — why it is asked, what a\nsufficient answer contains, a worked example, and what the answer changes — so\nanswering does not require prior knowledge of this workflow.\n\nAnswers are recorded with attribution. `--answered-by human` and\n`--answered-by roadmap` satisfy the gate; `--answered-by assumption` records\nthat the agent supplied the answer without human confirmation and blocks `ready`\nuntil a human decides:\n\n```bash\nstrict-spec-cli generate <change-name> questions --resolve requirement-problem \\\n  --answer \"...\" --answered-by human\n```\n\n`ready` emits `requirement_intake` diagnostics and exits non-zero while any\ncanonical question is missing, still open, answered by assumption, or resolved\nwith no attribution at all. No questions-generator flag removes\nrequirement-kind entries.\n\nEnforcement splits into three layers, and it is worth being clear about which is\nwhich:\n\n| Layer | Mechanism | Guarantees |\n|---|---|---|\n| CLI | first questions authoring, `ready` diagnostics | The questions exist, are answered, and carry attribution |\n| Skills | The asking technique in the four skill prompts, enforced by `validate-skills` | The questions are asked one at a time, carrying a concrete guess, explained before they are posed, with pushback that states its reason |\n| Neither | — | Whether the agent genuinely interrogated. This is judgment and cannot be checked; recording `answered_by: assumption` makes an evasion visible rather than preventing it |\n\n## Scene-First Requirement Routing\n\nRequirement interrogation establishes the *need*. Scene-first routing\nestablishes the *situation the need occurs in*, and it runs between the two\ngates above and below, because a requirement set written without it has holes\nthat are invisible in the requirements themselves.\n\n`/strict-spec-propose`, `/strict-spec-brainstorm`, and `/strict-spec-auto` ask\nfour questions in every proposal, whatever the change turns out to be: who acts\nand what each actor does, what must already be true before the main flow begins,\nwhat happens step by ordered step when nothing goes wrong, and what can go\nwrong, what triggers each exception flow, and what happens then. An author asked\nfor requirements immediately skips all four and never notices, because nothing\nin a requirement records the question that was never asked.\n\nThe scene is **mandatory as a question and optional as an artifact**. When the\nchange touches user-observable behavior, the scene is written down — as a delta\nscene under the change, before the delta spec, so each requirement is derived\nfrom a flow step rather than recalled. When the change is purely technical\n— a refactor, build or test tooling, repository maintenance, an architecture\nconstraint, a script, or skill guidance — there is no actor flow to describe,\nand the skill records why no scene applies instead of inventing one.\n\nEvery flow step declares either the requirements that cover it or an explicit\nreason it is deliberately covered by none, and one step may name covering\nrequirements across several spec areas. Once a change carries a delta scene,\n`ready` and `verify` exit non-zero on a step naming neither, and the diagnostic\nnames the runnable command that records the coverage:\n\n```bash\nstrict-spec-cli scene-status --scene <scene-id>\nstrict-spec-cli generate <change-name> delta-scene --target-scene <scene-id> \\\n  --operation MODIFIED --step <step-id> --action \"...\" \\\n  --covered-by <spec-id>:<requirement-id>\n```\n\nChecking is triggered by the presence of a scene, never by the presence of a\nspec. A project that records no scene sees no new diagnostic and no new required\nfield, so adopting scenes is incremental and upgrading the CLI is not a breaking\nchange. Scenes live under `.strict-spec-driven/scenes/<area>/`, are edited\noutside a change with `/strict-scene-edit`, and are merged into that store by\n`archive` when a change carries delta scenes.\n\n## Change Intake Question Gate\n\nProposal-oriented skills run a short change-intake gate after the requirement\ninterrogation above and before implementation.\nThey first answer the checklist from available evidence: the active request,\nroadmap planned-change details, strict specs, technical\nstandards, existing change artifacts, repository search results, and\ndeterministic CLI output. If that evidence answers the checklist, the workflow\ndoes not ask the human again.\n\nThe checklist covers what is changing, why it matters, what is included, what is\nleft out, what existing behavior must stay the same, where the work likely\nlives, how it will be proven, relevant risks or dependencies, and which\ntechnical rules apply.\n\nWhen a required answer is still missing after discovery, the skill records an\nentry in `questions.yaml.open` with concrete context, impact, and a recommended\nanswer or options. Implementation handoff intentionally stops while\n`questions.yaml.open` contains required entries; the answers must be resolved\ninto `questions.yaml.resolved` before `/strict-spec-auto` or\n`/strict-spec-apply` continues.\n\nHuman-facing question text, context, recommendations, options, and summaries\nuse `.strict-spec-driven/config.yaml` `preferred_human_language` unless the\nactive user message asks for a different language. Stable technical values such\nas question ids, artifact fields, schema names, command names, and file paths\nremain language-neutral identifiers.\n\n### MCP Server: `strict-spec-driven-mcp`\n\nThe built package also includes a local MCP server for MCP-compatible clients. It is a stdio-only integration and always requires an explicit project root:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"strict-spec-driven\": {\n      \"command\": \"node\",\n      \"args\": [\n        \"/path/to/strict-spec-driven/dist/scripts/strict-spec-driven-mcp.js\",\n        \"--project\",\n        \"/path/to/your/project\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Registering the server with `mcp-install`\n\nInstead of editing client config by hand, the `mcp-install` command builds the package and registers the server into supported clients:\n\n```bash\nstrict-spec-cli mcp-install --client claude-code --client codex --scope project\nstrict-spec-cli mcp-install --client all --project /path/to/your/project\nstrict-spec-cli mcp-install --client json --config ./.mcp.json\n```\n\n- `--client` accepts `claude-code`, `codex`, `opencode`, `copilot`, `json`, or `all`, and may be repeated. `all` expands to the four named clients; `json` is opt-in because it requires an explicit `--config` path.\n- `claude-code` and `codex` are registered through their official CLIs (`claude mcp add`, `codex mcp add`); when that CLI is not on `PATH` the client is skipped with a diagnostic rather than hand-writing its config.\n- `opencode` (`mcp` key), `copilot` (`.vscode/mcp.json` `servers` key), and `json` (`mcpServers` key, written to `--config`) are merged idempotently in each client's native format, preserving existing entries.\n- `--scope` selects `project` (default) or `user` config targets, `--project` sets the registered project root (defaults to the current directory), and `--name` overrides the entry name (default `strict-spec-driven`).\n\nThe MCP server preserves the CLI and Agent Skills as the authoritative workflow implementation. It exposes strict workflow artifacts as resources, delegates tools to the strict CLI under the configured project root, and provides prompts that guide existing strict skills.\n\nRepresentative resources:\n- `strict://workflow/state`\n- `strict://changes/{change}/proposal`\n- `strict://specs/{specId}`\n- `strict://roadmap/planned-changes/{plannedChangeId}`\n\nRepresentative read-only request: ask the MCP client to check strict workflow status. The expected tool call is `check_workflow_state`, which delegates to `strict-spec-cli check-workflow-state` and does not mutate project files.\n\nRepresentative prompt request: ask for `strict_review_change` with `{ \"change\": \"my-change\" }` to get review guidance that keeps verification, direct code review, and complete regression evidence in the workflow.\n\nMutating tools require explicit confirmation. Every mutating tool enforces a server-side gate: the call must include `confirm: true`, or the MCP server refuses it with a confirmation-required diagnostic and never invokes the strict CLI. Tools such as `propose_change`, `generate_change_artifact`, `mark_next_task_complete`, `roadmap_sync`, and `archive_change` write strict workflow state. `archive_change` is release-class because it merges specs and reconciles roadmap state, and `cancel_change` is destructive because it permanently deletes an active change. Tool failures (invalid input, missing confirmation, or a non-zero delegated command) are returned as MCP protocol errors while still carrying the diagnostic JSON envelope.\n\nTemplate resources are enumerable: an MCP client can list resources to discover the concrete `strict://` URIs for existing changes, specs, roadmap milestones, planned changes, and tech standards instead of having to know each identifier in advance.\n\n## CLI-Only YAML Authoring\n\nCLI code owns YAML shape and filesystem mutation for every repository-owned strict artifact. A supported AI workflow expresses each creation, update, named-entry removal, and whole-artifact deletion through the owning strict CLI command with named semantic arguments; it never supplies raw YAML payloads, heredocs, temporary payload files, arbitrary output paths, or direct file edits. Read-only YAML inspection is always allowed, and humans keep full manual autonomy — the policy governs AI mutation workflows, not people.\n\nThe canonical statement of the protocol is emitted by the CLI itself:\n\n```sh\nstrict-spec-cli yaml-authoring-protocol\nstrict-spec-cli yaml-authoring-protocol <artifact-kind>\n```\n\nIts seven ordered steps:\n\n1. **Read the owning command's machine-readable help** — `<owning-command> --help --json`. Help is the contract; a remembered flag is not.\n2. **Resolve the target artifact's schema** — `schema-list`, then `schema-show <schema-key>`.\n3. **Inspect the registered minimal example** — `sample-list`, then `sample-show <example-key>`. The example is read-only reference, never copied into place as a starting file.\n4. **Translate confirmed facts into documented named arguments** — every value must trace to the request, the schema, or repository evidence.\n5. **Execute the owning command with those arguments.**\n6. **Inspect the structured result and changed paths** — a result reporting incompleteness is a successful write with more work to do, not a failure to retry.\n7. **Run the artifact's validation command** — which catches cross-file consequences a single mutation's own result cannot report.\n\nThe protocol ends with one boundary: when a required command, argument, or artifact kind is unavailable, **stop and report the missing capability** — the operation that could not be completed, the artifact kind and target, the missing command or argument, and the discovery commands already consulted. There is no fallback; an unavailable command is a reason to stop, not a reason to improvise.\n\nDiscover the full mutation surface per artifact family with `yaml-surface-list` and `yaml-surface-show <kind>`, which report each family's supported operations, owning commands, refused operations, and cross-file effects. Artifact writes are lazy and atomic: the first successful `generate` call creates the change container and artifact together, no placeholder files are ever written, and a rejected write leaves pre-existing state untouched.\n\n## Schema and Example Assets\n\nBundled strict YAML schema files live under `templates/strict-spec-driven/schemas/`. Minimal valid examples live under `examples/strict-spec-driven/` as repository reference assets. These are separate from `.strict-spec-driven/` workflow state.\n\nThe schema registry at `templates/strict-spec-driven/schemas/schema-registry.yaml` indexes all supported strict artifact families.\n\nThe CLI also embeds bundled schema and scaffold assets so installed skills do not need a per-skill `templates` symlink. Agents can inspect embedded contracts with:\n\n```sh\nstrict-spec-cli schema-list\nstrict-spec-cli schema-show change-proposal\nstrict-spec-cli sample-show change-proposal\nstrict-spec-cli template-show scaffold/config.yaml\n```\n\n### Shared Schema Cache\n\nOn startup, every `strict-spec-driven` CLI command (except `--help`/`-h`) and the MCP server export the bundled schema files and strict YAML sample files to a shared on-disk cache at `~/.strict-spec-schemas/`, so agents can resolve an artifact's `schema_file` path and schema `example_ref` paths against one well-known root without per-project `templates` or `examples` directories. The cache holds the schema files under `templates/strict-spec-driven/schemas/`, the sample files under `examples/strict-spec-driven/`, and a `manifest.yaml` recording the exported version; it is refreshed automatically when bundled schemas or samples change or a cached file is missing or modified, and it is always safe to delete — the next run recreates it. Set the `STRICT_SPEC_SCHEMA_CACHE_DIR` environment variable to relocate the cache root. If the cache cannot be written, the command continues with bundled assets and prints a warning naming the resolved cache path. An artifact's declared `schema_file` path resolves beneath this cache root, and a schema's `example_ref` paths resolve beneath the cached examples tree; installed skills never assume a source-repository `templates` or `examples` directory.\n\n## Standard Operating Procedures\n\nSchema-validated standard operating procedures live under `.strict-spec-driven/sops/` and are CLI-owned YAML. Author and maintain them through named arguments — `sop-create` for a new procedure (`--title`, `--category`, `--purpose`, `--trigger`, steps, roles, escalations, exceptions), `sop-edit` for field and named-entry updates and removals, and `sop-validate` to validate one or all procedures against the SOP schema. Inspect read-only with `sop-list`, `sop-show`, `sop-category-list`, and `sop-category-show`; discover the artifact contract with `schema-show sop` and `schema-show sop-category`.\n\n```sh\nstrict-spec-cli sop-create --title \"Release rollback\" --category release \\\n  --purpose \"...\" --trigger \"...\" --scope-in \"...\" --success \"...\" \\\n  --prerequisite \"...\" --exception-condition \"...\" --exception-handling \"...\" \\\n  --step-id assess --step-actor operator --step-action \"...\" --step-expected \"...\"\nstrict-spec-cli sop-edit <sop-id> --step-id verify --step-actor operator \\\n  --step-action \"...\" --step-expected \"...\"\nstrict-spec-cli sop-validate\n```\n\nEach category enforces its own required sections — `sop-category-show <id>` names them before you author.\n\n## Migration Boundaries\n\nThe `strict-spec-cli migrate` subcommand converts supported legacy `.spec-driven/` Markdown artifacts into `.strict-spec-driven/` YAML artifacts. The source `.spec-driven/` directory is not modified. Unsupported or ambiguous legacy shapes are reported as manual follow-up items in the migration report.\n\nAfter migration, `.strict-spec-driven/` is the active workflow state. `.spec-driven/` remains as bootstrap or legacy history and is not treated as a valid strict workflow target.\n\n## dsh Document Viewer Plugin\n\n`plugins/dsh-strict-spec/` holds `dsh-plugin-strict-spec`, a read-only DeepSeek Harness plugin that browses a workspace's `.strict-spec-driven/` documents in the web client and the Desktop app. See its [README](plugins/dsh-strict-spec/README.md) for the one-command install from npm (`dsh plugin --profile web add dsh-plugin-strict-spec`), the `link:` and locally packed tarball development paths, and the supported dsh releases. The package is published to npm under the Apache-2.0 license.\n\n## Self-Hosting Status\n\nThis repository is self-hosted on the strict workflow. `.strict-spec-driven/` is the single active workflow state, containing the active strict specs, roadmap, and change history. Migration from the legacy `.spec-driven/` workflow is complete, and no root `.spec-driven/` directory is retained.\n\n## Development\n\n**Prerequisites:** [Bun](https://bun.sh) is the runtime and package manager (the installer installs it automatically when missing). Node.js is only needed for the `node`-based commands (`bun run validate-skills`, the MCP server) — not for installation.\n\n**Build:**\n```sh\nbun run build\n```\nCompiles `src/` to `dist/` via TypeScript and copies `templates/` and `scripts/` into `dist/`.\n\n**Test:**\n```sh\nbun test/run.ts\n```\nRuns the test suite against compiled `dist/` output.\n\n**Git hooks (one-time per clone):**\n```sh\ngit config core.hooksPath .githooks\n```\nEnables the pre-commit freshness gate: commits touching bundle inputs (`src/`, `templates/`, `package.json`, `bun.lock`) are rebuilt and rejected when the committed `dist/scripts` bundles or `src/embedded-templates.ts` are stale, since git-dependency consumers install the pre-built bundles as committed.\n\n**Validate skills:**\n```sh\nbun run validate-skills\n```\nValidates all strict skill packages for metadata, command references, and required command script access.\n\n**Conventions:**\n- Use a structured YAML parser — never ad-hoc string processing for core artifact data.\n- Spec changes should be reflected in `.strict-spec-driven/specs/` before implementation.\n- Run `bun run build && bun test/run.ts` after any source change.\n- Read existing code before modifying it; implement only what the current task requires.\n"
